# 1Password env-file template — `op run --env-file=.env.op -- <cmd>` resolves
# the op:// references to actual secret values and injects them as env vars
# for the wrapped command. Values never appear in shell scrollback or argv.
#
# This file IS committed (it contains references, not secrets). Anyone with
# read access to the Goldberry Grove - Admin vault's Grove Infra
# item gets the values; anyone without it gets `op` errors and nothing leaks.

# Provider credentials (read by TF as TF_VAR_*)
TF_VAR_do_token=op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/do_token

# ACCOUNT-scoped Cloudflare token (DNS + Zone read + Zone Settings + SSL and
# Certificates edit on all four brand zones). The SSL permission authorizes
# cloudflare_origin_ca_certificate - Cloudflare deprecated the legacy Origin
# CA Key, so origin certs issue via this token too.
TF_VAR_cloudflare_api_token=op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/account_cloudflare_api_token

# Spaces "plumbing" key (All Buckets) — provider-side bucket ops ONLY.
# The droplet gets its own TF-created bucket-scoped key (blogs.tf); this
# one never lands on a droplet.
TF_VAR_spaces_access_id=op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/spaces_bootstrap_access_key_id
TF_VAR_spaces_secret_key=op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/spaces_bootstrap_secret_key

# S3 backend auth (read by the AWS SDK that the TF S3 backend uses for Spaces)
AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/spaces_bootstrap_access_key_id
AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/spaces_bootstrap_secret_key

# --- App Platform frontends (GOL-116 / GOL-286) -----------------------------
# LIVE as of 2026-07-26 (GOL-817 App Platform apply). All three fields exist in
# Grove Infra (Admin vault):
#   * grove_revalidate_secret:    32-byte hex, shared across all 4 apps.
#   * odoo_api_keys_tf_json:      JSON map {"goldberry":..,"ggg":..,"nursery":..},
#                                 global-scope res.users.apikeys on the PROD Odoo.
#   * ghost_content_keys_tf_json: JSON map {"hub":..,"goldberry":..,"ggg":..,
#                                 "nursery":..}, assembled from the per-blog
#                                 ghost_content_key_* fields (GOL-817).
TF_VAR_grove_revalidate_secret=op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/grove_revalidate_secret
TF_VAR_odoo_api_keys=op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/odoo_api_keys_tf_json
TF_VAR_ghost_content_keys=op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/ghost_content_keys_tf_json

# Mailgun transactional SMTP for the 4 Ghost blogs (GOL-517 / GOL-248 cutover).
#   * ghost_smtp_tf_json: JSON map {"hub":{"user","pass","from"},"goldberry":..,
#     "ggg":..,"nursery":..}. Per-tenant grove-tx@<sending-domain> SMTP creds,
#     validated live against smtp.mailgun.org:587 (2026-08-05). NOTE the hub
#     tenant sends from `send.gatheringatthegrove.com` (the provisioned+verified
#     Mailgun domain), NOT `mg.gatheringatthegrove.com` — the variable `from`
#     absorbs that, so no config change is needed. Populated in 1Password by an
#     admin (the op service account is read-only on this vault). Until the field
#     exists, `op run` errors loudly — that is intentional: an empty ghost_smtp
#     must not silently ship. Pre-populate leaves the transport INERT because
#     staffDeviceVerification stays false (below), so a populated map alone is a
#     safe, no-lockout first stage (magic links + receipts flow; staff logins
#     unaffected).
TF_VAR_ghost_smtp=op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/ghost_smtp_tf_json
# staffDeviceVerification is the ONLY lockout-risk flag: with it true, a broken
# SMTP transport 500s every Ghost staff login. It is deliberately NOT wired here
# so a routine `op run ... apply` keeps it false (the variable default). Flip it
# only as an explicit, post-verification cutover step:
#   TF_VAR_ghost_staff_device_verification=true op run --env-file=.env.op -- terraform apply ...
# See docs/RUNBOOK-mailgun-transactional-email.md.

# Discord #grove-ops paging webhook for the platform-plane alerts (GOL-381).
# observability.tf appends Discord's `/slack` Slack-compat suffix itself.
TF_VAR_discord_webhook_url=op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/discord_webhook_url

# --- Nightly BLOGS backup dead-man's switch + user_data reproducibility ------
#     (GOL-385 acceptance #2 / GOL-1192)
# var.healthchecks_ping_url feeds the blogs droplet's cloud-init user_data, and
# the DO provider stores user_data as a SHA1 -- so the value is NOT merely a
# backup-ping setting, it is a user_data input. The default is "" (pings off),
# but the LIVE grove-prod-blogs was applied with a value that lives ONLY in an
# operator-local terraform.tfvars, so its user_data is not reproducible from the
# repo alone. That is the remaining half of GOL-385 (#2 reproducibility); the
# ignore_changes mask on digitalocean_droplet.blogs only HIDES this drift, it
# does not reconcile it. This block is the codified home for that input so a
# fresh rebuild comes up as the same droplet. To close (see
# docs/RUNBOOK-blogs-user_data-reproducibility.md):
#   1. Recover the real Healthchecks blogs-backup ping URL from the operator who
#      ran the prod apply (op-local tfvars). "" if pings were intentionally off.
#   2. Store it (op SA is read-only here, so an admin runs this):
#        op item edit "Grove Infra" --vault "Goldberry Grove - Admin" \
#          healthchecks_ping_url="https://hc-ping.com/<uuid>"
#   3. Uncomment the line below. NOTE: this URL is one of THREE user_data changes
#      bundled into the pending blogs cutover replace (the others -- blog.* URL
#      flip GOL-387 and Mailgun SMTP arm GOL-517 -- are already codified). After
#      that board-gated replace, a clean plan with the ignore_changes mask lifted
#      must show NO unexplained blogs replace.
#   RECOVERED + CODIFIED 2026-08-07 (GOL-1192 step 1+2): the operator confirmed
#   the real Healthchecks blogs-backup ping URL and it is now stored in 1P at
#   Grove Infra/healthchecks_ping_url. Step 3 (verify a mask-lifted plan shows no
#   UNEXPLAINED blogs replace) is the remaining acceptance item.
TF_VAR_healthchecks_ping_url=op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/healthchecks_ping_url

# --- Nightly Odoo FILESTORE backup dead-man's switch (GOL-99 / GOL-382) ------
# ARMED 2026-08-08 (GOL-830). The Healthchecks.io check exists and its ping URL
# is stored in the Grove Infra field `odoo_backup_healthchecks_ping_url` (an
# admin populated it; the op service account is read-only on this vault). This
# is a SEPARATE check from the blogs one — one check per job, so a green blogs
# ping can't mask a dead Odoo backup (the exact failure a dead-man's switch
# exists to catch). The script pings only on success, so a silent failure trips
# the check.
#
# NOTE: the URL is baked into the odoo droplet cloud-init user_data, so this
# line's first `apply` FORCES A DROPLET REPLACE (production-affecting —
# board-approved change window; the durable filestore volume survives, validated
# on QA L3 under GOL-825 and re-checked at replace time via
# scripts/check-attachment-invariant.sh, the GOL-93 launch gate).
TF_VAR_odoo_backup_healthchecks_ping_url=op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/odoo_backup_healthchecks_ping_url

# --- Prod checkout Stripe keys — DEDICATED backend key (GOL-973) -------------
# COMMENTED until CFO (Penny) mints the live keys and the board greenlights a
# prod-checkout rebuild. Empty defaults in variables.tf keep plan/apply working
# and checkout inert until then; user_data is in ignore_changes, so activating
# these requires a board-gated droplet REBUILD (rides GOL-920), not just a
# re-apply.
#
# Runbook §4 (docs/RUNBOOK-checkout-stripe-guardrails.md): these MUST resolve
# from their OWN 1Password item — NEVER a storefront key. QA's accepted
# shortcut of sharing one stripe-nursery-qa key between storefront + backend is
# a revenue-outage trap in prod (revoke the storefront key → checkout dies).
# The op:// paths below point at a dedicated backend item, distinct from any
# storefront item, by construction.
#
# To arm (when greenlit):
#   1. CFO mints a restricted LIVE key scoped to Checkout Session create +
#      webhook ops only (live equivalent of the QA rk_test_ scope, GOL-956),
#      and registers a prod Stripe webhook endpoint → prod grove_headless.
#   2. Create the dedicated item + fields:
#        op item create --category "API Credential" --vault "Goldberry Grove - Admin" \
#          --title "stripe-checkout-backend-prod" \
#          secret_key="rk_live_..." webhook_secret="whsec_..."
#   3. Uncomment the two lines below.
#   4. Board-approved rebuild + apply, then runbook §5 smoke test against prod.
# TF_VAR_stripe_test_secret_key=op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/stripe-checkout-backend-prod/secret_key
# TF_VAR_stripe_test_webhook_secret=op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/stripe-checkout-backend-prod/webhook_secret

# --- Grove assets (ADR-009 / GOL-1122) --------------------------------------
# The hub's /api/assets/optimize + the social re-host seam read these at request
# time. Operator key is minted by environments/assets
# (digitalocean_spaces_key.assets_rw) and pushed to Grove Infra by hand — the
# 1P field names deliberately differ from the env var names, don't "fix" them:
#   grove_assets_access_key_id -> GROVE_ASSETS_KEY
#   grove_assets_secret_key    -> GROVE_ASSETS_SECRET
TF_VAR_grove_assets_key=op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/grove_assets_access_key_id
TF_VAR_grove_assets_secret=op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/grove_assets_secret_key
TF_VAR_grove_assets_optimize_token=op://Goldberry Grove - Admin/Grove Infra/grove_assets_optimize_token
